WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.360
In the 19th century, the German philosopher
Arthur Schopenhauer read about Buddhism,

2
00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:07.740
and felt he had found an ally.

3
00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:09.900
He was a famous pessimist.

4
00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:12.140
He thought the Buddha agreed with him.

5
00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:15.810
After all, the Buddha's first
teaching begins with dukkha,

6
00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:18.170
often translated as suffering.

7
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.620
And even pleasure counts as dukkha.

8
00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.820
So if even a happy day
counts as suffering,

9
00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:26.570
is Buddhism a gloomy religion?

10
00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:30.820
In this mission, you'll see why no
single English word fits dukkha,

11
00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:33.100
and meet its three kinds.

12
00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:36.340
Then you'll watch
impermanence at two speeds:

13
00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:39.960
a tree's whole life, and a single moment.

14
00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:45.010
Your mission: weigh up whether the Buddha's
teaching on suffering really is gloomy.
